{"name":"Infinite Inkwell","hex":"#2f1064","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"infinite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Indefinably large, countlessly great; immense. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Boundless, endless, without end or limits; innumerable."]},{"part":"Numeral","definitions":["Infinitely many."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/infinite"},{"term":"inkwell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A container for ink, designed and usually positioned so that a person may conveniently dip a pen (such as a dip pen or quill pen) into it whenever a refill is needed.","A store of ink built into a pen, comprising a tube attached to the tip of the pen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inkwell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Inkwell","blocks":[{"text":"An inkwell is a small jar or container, often made of glass, porcelain, silver, brass, or pewter, used for holding ink in a place convenient for the person who is writing. The artist or writer dips the brush, quill, or dip pen into the inkwell as needed or uses the inkwell as the source for filling the reservoir of a fountain pen. An inkwell usually has a lid to prevent contamination, evaporation, accidental spillage, and excessive exposure to air. A type known as the travelling inkwell was fitted with a secure screw lid so a traveller could carry a supply of ink in their luggage without the risk of leakage."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Inkwell15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inkwell","wikidata":"Q1160546"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Inktpot_met_schroefdeksel_van_het_merk_Iridinoid_-_INDUS_V09588.JPG/250px-Inktpot_met_schroefdeksel_van_het_merk_Iridinoid_-_INDUS_V09588.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://t